1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,531
Last night I told you about V-E

2
00:00:02,531 --> 00:00:05,389
Day, the night every church bell in

3
00:00:05,389 --> 00:00:07,267
Mansfield rang at once.

4
00:00:07,447 --> 00:00:10,047
And about Eddie Pruitt sitting on our

5
00:00:10,047 --> 00:00:12,506
porch step at midnight with his bad

6
00:00:12,506 --> 00:00:13,560
arm in his lap,

7
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,090
telling my mother that my father was

8
00:00:16,090 --> 00:00:16,933
coming home.

9
00:00:17,113 --> 00:00:19,052
And then the second thing.

10
00:00:19,232 --> 00:00:21,403
Walt said to tell you,

11
00:00:21,403 --> 00:00:21,797
Mrs.

12
00:00:21,977 --> 00:00:22,686
Miller.

13
00:00:22,866 --> 00:00:24,363
He can count too.

14
00:00:24,543 --> 00:00:27,055
Emma, you've been thinking about that all

15
00:00:27,055 --> 00:00:28,096
day, haven't you?

16
00:00:28,276 --> 00:00:29,321
So have I.

17
00:00:29,501 --> 00:00:31,045
For eighty years.

18
00:00:31,395 --> 00:00:34,471
Tonight is August of nineteen forty-five.

19
00:00:34,651 --> 00:00:36,985
The month the war ended for good.

20
00:00:37,165 --> 00:00:38,813
The month I turned one.

21
00:00:38,993 --> 00:00:41,334
And the month my father came home

22
00:00:41,334 --> 00:00:43,392
on the train and looked at me

23
00:00:43,392 --> 00:00:45,945
like he was trying to remember where

24
00:00:45,945 --> 00:00:47,364
he'd seen me before.

25
00:00:47,544 --> 00:00:49,634
Come here, both of you.

26
00:00:49,814 --> 00:00:51,637
This one is hard to tell.

27
00:00:51,987 --> 00:00:54,181
All that summer my mother waited.

28
00:00:54,361 --> 00:00:56,514
She cleaned the house like the King

29
00:00:56,514 --> 00:00:57,867
of England was coming.

30
00:00:58,047 --> 00:01:00,202
She took in my father's one good

31
00:01:00,202 --> 00:01:02,828
suit, because Pearl said the Army would

32
00:01:02,828 --> 00:01:04,107
have made him thin.

33
00:01:04,287 --> 00:01:06,441
She practiced, Pearl told me,

34
00:01:06,441 --> 00:01:08,595
in front of the mirror in the

35
00:01:08,595 --> 00:01:08,966
hall.

36
00:01:09,146 --> 00:01:11,004
Practiced smiling.

37
00:01:11,184 --> 00:01:14,043
Practiced saying, welcome home,

38
00:01:14,043 --> 00:01:14,504
Walt.

39
00:01:14,684 --> 00:01:15,879
Over and over,

40
00:01:15,879 --> 00:01:17,842
until it sounded right.

41
00:01:18,193 --> 00:01:20,264
She never said a word to anyone

42
00:01:20,264 --> 00:01:21,734
about Eddie's message.

43
00:01:21,914 --> 00:01:23,063
Not to Pearl.

44
00:01:23,243 --> 00:01:24,439
Not to Frank.

45
00:01:24,619 --> 00:01:26,267
But she stopped eating.

46
00:01:26,447 --> 00:01:28,749
By July her dresses hung on her

47
00:01:28,749 --> 00:01:30,011
like on a hanger,

48
00:01:30,011 --> 00:01:32,238
and the ladies at church said,

49
00:01:32,238 --> 00:01:34,465
poor Dorothy, it's the nerves,

50
00:01:34,465 --> 00:01:36,246
waiting for her husband.

51
00:01:36,426 --> 00:01:37,645
And Mrs.

52
00:01:37,825 --> 00:01:40,658
Pruitt nodded and said nothing at all.

53
00:01:40,838 --> 00:01:42,945
For once in her life,

54
00:01:42,945 --> 00:01:43,346
Mrs.

55
00:01:43,526 --> 00:01:45,093
Pruitt said nothing.

56
00:01:45,444 --> 00:01:47,464
And Frank stopped coming.

57
00:01:47,644 --> 00:01:49,223
Just like that.

58
00:01:49,403 --> 00:01:50,585
After V-E Day,

59
00:01:50,585 --> 00:01:53,371
he didn't come by in the evenings

60
00:01:53,371 --> 00:01:54,047
anymore.

61
00:01:54,227 --> 00:01:55,391
No more coal,

62
00:01:55,391 --> 00:01:56,734
no more butter,

63
00:01:56,734 --> 00:01:58,615
no more Mairzy Doats.

64
00:01:58,795 --> 00:02:00,927
He'd wave from his car if he

65
00:02:00,927 --> 00:02:02,754
drove down Maple Street.

66
00:02:02,934 --> 00:02:05,474
Pearl said that frightened her more than

67
00:02:05,474 --> 00:02:06,046
anything.

68
00:02:06,226 --> 00:02:08,476
A man who stops coming around,

69
00:02:08,476 --> 00:02:10,577
she said, is a man who knows

70
00:02:10,577 --> 00:02:12,077
something is coming.

71
00:02:12,427 --> 00:02:15,103
Then, at the end of July,

72
00:02:15,103 --> 00:02:16,281
a telegram.

73
00:02:16,461 --> 00:02:18,479
But this time the boy on the

74
00:02:18,479 --> 00:02:19,921
bicycle was smiling.

75
00:02:20,101 --> 00:02:22,114
My mother opened it on the porch

76
00:02:22,114 --> 00:02:23,561
with her hands shaking.

77
00:02:23,741 --> 00:02:27,361
Arriving Mansfield Monday August six,

78
00:02:27,361 --> 00:02:28,536
two-forty P.

79
00:02:28,716 --> 00:02:29,377
M.

80
00:02:29,557 --> 00:02:30,300
train.

81
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:31,693
All my love,

82
00:02:31,693 --> 00:02:32,199
Walt.

83
00:02:32,379 --> 00:02:33,751
All my love,

84
00:02:33,751 --> 00:02:34,666
it said.

85
00:02:34,846 --> 00:02:37,214
My mother read those three words a

86
00:02:37,214 --> 00:02:38,189
hundred times.

87
00:02:38,369 --> 00:02:39,809
All my love.

88
00:02:39,989 --> 00:02:41,173
Did it mean it?

89
00:02:41,353 --> 00:02:42,445
Did he know?

90
00:02:42,795 --> 00:02:44,930
Pearl got the white dress out,

91
00:02:44,930 --> 00:02:47,137
the one she'd sewn from a flour

92
00:02:47,137 --> 00:02:48,774
sack for my photograph.

93
00:02:48,954 --> 00:02:51,094
I'd grown since February,

94
00:02:51,094 --> 00:02:53,319
so she let out every seam.

95
00:02:53,499 --> 00:02:56,242
And on Monday morning my mother curled

96
00:02:56,242 --> 00:02:58,623
my hair, what little there was of

97
00:02:58,623 --> 00:03:01,005
it, with her fingers and a little

98
00:03:01,005 --> 00:03:03,747
sugar water, which is what mothers did

99
00:03:03,747 --> 00:03:05,840
then, and put a ribbon in it.

100
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:07,855
I looked, Pearl said,

101
00:03:07,855 --> 00:03:09,515
like a little cake.

102
00:03:09,865 --> 00:03:12,112
The station was full that day,

103
00:03:12,112 --> 00:03:13,011
sweethearts.

104
00:03:13,191 --> 00:03:16,215
Every week in the summer of forty-five,

105
00:03:16,215 --> 00:03:19,161
trains came in full of soldiers coming

106
00:03:19,161 --> 00:03:21,797
home, and half the town would turn

107
00:03:21,797 --> 00:03:22,107
out.

108
00:03:22,287 --> 00:03:25,891
Mothers, wives, girlfriends with new hats,

109
00:03:25,891 --> 00:03:28,893
little brothers who'd grown a foot.

110
00:03:29,073 --> 00:03:31,794
A band from the American Legion played

111
00:03:31,794 --> 00:03:32,939
on the platform.

112
00:03:33,119 --> 00:03:35,385
And then you heard the whistle,

113
00:03:35,385 --> 00:03:36,700
way down the line,

114
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:39,331
and everyone pushed forward at once.

115
00:03:39,681 --> 00:03:42,394
My father was almost the last man

116
00:03:42,394 --> 00:03:42,722
off.

117
00:03:42,902 --> 00:03:44,772
Pearl saw him first.

118
00:03:44,952 --> 00:03:47,475
He came down the steps slowly,

119
00:03:47,475 --> 00:03:48,484
with a cane,

120
00:03:48,484 --> 00:03:51,260
sideways, the way you do when one

121
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:53,195
leg doesn't bend right.

122
00:03:53,375 --> 00:03:55,792
He was so thin his uniform looked

123
00:03:55,792 --> 00:03:56,451
borrowed.

124
00:03:56,631 --> 00:03:58,927
And there was a white scar across

125
00:03:58,927 --> 00:04:00,874
his forehead, into his hair,

126
00:04:00,874 --> 00:04:04,143
that hadn't been there in nineteen forty-three.

127
00:04:04,323 --> 00:04:06,088
Pearl said, oh,

128
00:04:06,088 --> 00:04:06,912
my boy.

129
00:04:07,092 --> 00:04:08,520
Oh, my boy.

130
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:09,873
And she ran.

131
00:04:10,223 --> 00:04:12,441
He held his mother for a long

132
00:04:12,441 --> 00:04:12,823
time.

133
00:04:13,003 --> 00:04:14,394
Then he looked up,

134
00:04:14,394 --> 00:04:15,784
over her shoulder,

135
00:04:15,784 --> 00:04:16,788
at my mother.

136
00:04:16,968 --> 00:04:19,550
She was standing very still with me

137
00:04:19,550 --> 00:04:21,837
on her hip in that white dress,

138
00:04:21,837 --> 00:04:24,788
smiling the smile she'd practiced in the

139
00:04:24,788 --> 00:04:25,304
mirror.

140
00:04:25,484 --> 00:04:27,852
And my father looked at her.

141
00:04:28,032 --> 00:04:29,751
And then he looked at me.

142
00:04:29,931 --> 00:04:31,192
And Pearl said,

143
00:04:31,192 --> 00:04:32,706
for just a second,

144
00:04:32,706 --> 00:04:34,052
he didn't smile.

145
00:04:34,232 --> 00:04:35,486
Then he did.

146
00:04:35,836 --> 00:04:38,169
He kissed my mother on the cheek.

147
00:04:38,349 --> 00:04:39,764
Not on the mouth,

148
00:04:39,764 --> 00:04:40,845
on the cheek.

149
00:04:41,025 --> 00:04:43,466
And then he held out his arms

150
00:04:43,466 --> 00:04:44,056
for me.

151
00:04:44,236 --> 00:04:46,616
And sweethearts, I screamed.

152
00:04:46,796 --> 00:04:48,305
Like he was a bear.

153
00:04:48,485 --> 00:04:50,482
I'd never seen a man in a

154
00:04:50,482 --> 00:04:51,840
uniform up close,

155
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:54,157
and I'd never seen that face,

156
00:04:54,157 --> 00:04:57,192
and I screamed and grabbed my mother's

157
00:04:57,192 --> 00:04:59,189
neck and wouldn't let go.

158
00:04:59,369 --> 00:05:01,389
The whole platform laughed.

159
00:05:01,569 --> 00:05:03,404
My father laughed too.

160
00:05:03,584 --> 00:05:05,627
But he put his arms down.

161
00:05:05,977 --> 00:05:07,858
That first night at home,

162
00:05:07,858 --> 00:05:10,493
my mother made his favorite supper.

163
00:05:10,673 --> 00:05:13,905
Fried chicken, mashed potatoes and gravy,

164
00:05:13,905 --> 00:05:15,166
and a peach pie,

165
00:05:15,166 --> 00:05:18,161
with sugar she'd saved for two months.

166
00:05:18,341 --> 00:05:19,479
He ate a little.

167
00:05:19,659 --> 00:05:21,772
He said it was wonderful.

168
00:05:21,952 --> 00:05:23,984
He said it three times.

169
00:05:24,164 --> 00:05:26,155
Then he went out on the back

170
00:05:26,155 --> 00:05:27,222
porch to smoke,

171
00:05:27,222 --> 00:05:29,213
and he sat there in the dark

172
00:05:29,213 --> 00:05:30,706
until after midnight,

173
00:05:30,706 --> 00:05:32,128
looking at the yard.

174
00:05:32,479 --> 00:05:34,777
He slept on the porch that night,

175
00:05:34,777 --> 00:05:35,753
on the glider,

176
00:05:35,753 --> 00:05:36,797
with a blanket.

177
00:05:36,977 --> 00:05:38,990
He told my mother it was the

178
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:41,289
heat, and that he wasn't used to

179
00:05:41,289 --> 00:05:42,655
a soft bed anymore.

180
00:05:42,835 --> 00:05:45,180
And maybe that was true.

181
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:47,244
A lot of the boys who came

182
00:05:47,244 --> 00:05:49,853
home couldn't sleep inside at first.

183
00:05:50,033 --> 00:05:53,256
But Pearl lay awake upstairs listening to

184
00:05:53,256 --> 00:05:55,929
that glider squeak back and forth,

185
00:05:55,929 --> 00:05:57,108
back and forth,

186
00:05:57,108 --> 00:05:58,288
all night long.

187
00:05:58,638 --> 00:05:59,837
In the morning,

188
00:05:59,837 --> 00:06:01,756
he was up before anyone,

189
00:06:01,756 --> 00:06:02,875
making coffee.

190
00:06:03,055 --> 00:06:04,828
When I woke up in my crib

191
00:06:04,828 --> 00:06:07,025
he was standing in the doorway,

192
00:06:07,025 --> 00:06:08,373
just looking at me.

193
00:06:08,553 --> 00:06:10,143
Not saying anything.

194
00:06:10,323 --> 00:06:12,599
My mother came in behind him and

195
00:06:12,599 --> 00:06:13,168
stopped.

196
00:06:13,348 --> 00:06:14,557
And he said,

197
00:06:14,557 --> 00:06:17,983
very quietly, she's got your eyes,

198
00:06:17,983 --> 00:06:18,386
Dot.

199
00:06:18,566 --> 00:06:20,761
And my mother said yes.

200
00:06:20,941 --> 00:06:22,004
And he said,

201
00:06:22,004 --> 00:06:23,332
and whose chin?

202
00:06:23,512 --> 00:06:26,018
And he walked away to finish the

203
00:06:26,018 --> 00:06:26,566
coffee.

204
00:06:26,916 --> 00:06:28,956
On Tuesday he put on his uniform

205
00:06:28,956 --> 00:06:30,805
again and took the bus out to

206
00:06:30,805 --> 00:06:32,272
the Westinghouse plant,

207
00:06:32,272 --> 00:06:34,567
where they were hiring men back from

208
00:06:34,567 --> 00:06:35,077
the war.

209
00:06:35,257 --> 00:06:38,192
They were already changing the lines from

210
00:06:38,192 --> 00:06:40,768
war work back to electric ranges and

211
00:06:40,768 --> 00:06:44,418
refrigerators, because everyone in America wanted a

212
00:06:44,418 --> 00:06:45,277
new kitchen.

213
00:06:45,457 --> 00:06:47,264
He came home with a job on

214
00:06:47,264 --> 00:06:49,419
the line starting in September,

215
00:06:49,419 --> 00:06:50,879
a dollar ten an hour.

216
00:06:51,059 --> 00:06:53,080
He put the paper on the kitchen

217
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:54,449
table without a word.

218
00:06:54,799 --> 00:06:55,974
And that evening,

219
00:06:55,974 --> 00:06:58,325
Pearl saw him go across the street

220
00:06:58,325 --> 00:06:59,431
to the Pruitts'.

221
00:06:59,611 --> 00:07:01,810
He and Eddie sat on the Pruitts'

222
00:07:01,810 --> 00:07:03,459
porch steps for an hour,

223
00:07:03,459 --> 00:07:05,726
two soldiers, one with a cane and

224
00:07:05,726 --> 00:07:07,993
one with an arm that didn't work.

225
00:07:08,173 --> 00:07:10,402
Pearl couldn't hear what they said.

226
00:07:10,582 --> 00:07:12,928
But when my father came back across

227
00:07:12,928 --> 00:07:15,139
the street, he didn't come in the

228
00:07:15,139 --> 00:07:15,877
front door.

229
00:07:16,057 --> 00:07:17,601
He went around the back.

230
00:07:17,951 --> 00:07:19,506
That same afternoon,

231
00:07:19,506 --> 00:07:22,850
the radio said something nobody understood.

232
00:07:23,030 --> 00:07:26,387
President Truman said that sixteen hours before,

233
00:07:26,387 --> 00:07:29,254
an American airplane had dropped a single

234
00:07:29,254 --> 00:07:32,121
bomb on a Japanese city called Hiroshima.

235
00:07:32,301 --> 00:07:33,508
One bomb.

236
00:07:33,688 --> 00:07:35,933
And that it had more power than

237
00:07:35,933 --> 00:07:37,960
twenty thousand tons of TNT.

238
00:07:38,140 --> 00:07:39,428
An atomic bomb,

239
00:07:39,428 --> 00:07:40,544
he called it.

240
00:07:40,724 --> 00:07:43,674
My father turned off the radio and

241
00:07:43,674 --> 00:07:45,062
said, it's over,

242
00:07:45,062 --> 00:07:45,495
then.

243
00:07:45,675 --> 00:07:47,324
It's going to be over.

244
00:07:47,674 --> 00:07:51,143
Pearl said nobody on Maple Street celebrated

245
00:07:51,143 --> 00:07:51,853
that day.

246
00:07:52,033 --> 00:07:54,135
People didn't know what to think.

247
00:07:54,315 --> 00:07:55,528
A whole city,

248
00:07:55,528 --> 00:07:56,834
with one bomb.

249
00:07:57,014 --> 00:07:57,943
Mrs.

250
00:07:58,123 --> 00:08:00,361
Pruitt went to church in the middle

251
00:08:00,361 --> 00:08:01,128
of the week,

252
00:08:01,128 --> 00:08:02,407
which she never did.

253
00:08:02,587 --> 00:08:04,910
And my father sat at the kitchen

254
00:08:04,910 --> 00:08:07,450
table with the newspaper for hours,

255
00:08:07,450 --> 00:08:09,047
reading the same page,

256
00:08:09,047 --> 00:08:11,805
while my mother washed the same plate.

257
00:08:12,155 --> 00:08:14,256
And then came Wednesday.

258
00:08:14,436 --> 00:08:16,107
The eighth of August,

259
00:08:16,107 --> 00:08:17,698
nineteen forty-five.

260
00:08:17,878 --> 00:08:19,608
My first birthday.

261
00:08:19,788 --> 00:08:22,377
My mother had been saving sugar stamps

262
00:08:22,377 --> 00:08:24,693
since June, and she baked a little

263
00:08:24,693 --> 00:08:26,941
white cake with one candle in it,

264
00:08:26,941 --> 00:08:28,984
and Pearl put a paper crown on

265
00:08:28,984 --> 00:08:29,529
my head.

266
00:08:29,709 --> 00:08:31,485
The Pruitts came over.

267
00:08:31,665 --> 00:08:32,818
Eddie came too,

268
00:08:32,818 --> 00:08:34,510
and stood by the door,

269
00:08:34,510 --> 00:08:36,739
and wouldn't look at anybody.

270
00:08:37,089 --> 00:08:38,505
And Frank came.

271
00:08:38,685 --> 00:08:40,922
He hadn't been to the house since

272
00:08:40,922 --> 00:08:41,193
May.

273
00:08:41,373 --> 00:08:43,885
He walked in without knocking,

274
00:08:43,885 --> 00:08:45,727
the way he always had,

275
00:08:45,727 --> 00:08:48,072
with a huge box in his arms.

276
00:08:48,252 --> 00:08:50,748
Inside was a rocking horse.

277
00:08:50,928 --> 00:08:51,801
A real one,

278
00:08:51,801 --> 00:08:54,976
wooden, painted white with a red saddle,

279
00:08:54,976 --> 00:08:57,755
from the department store downtown.

280
00:08:57,935 --> 00:09:00,540
It must have cost twenty dollars,

281
00:09:00,540 --> 00:09:02,277
a week of a man's pay.

282
00:09:02,457 --> 00:09:04,976
For a baby who couldn't walk yet.

283
00:09:05,326 --> 00:09:06,916
Frank set me up on it,

284
00:09:06,916 --> 00:09:09,085
holding me so I wouldn't fall,

285
00:09:09,085 --> 00:09:11,759
and I laughed and laughed and grabbed

286
00:09:11,759 --> 00:09:12,988
the horse's ears.

287
00:09:13,168 --> 00:09:15,581
And everyone in the room looked at

288
00:09:15,581 --> 00:09:17,568
me laughing in Frank's arms.

289
00:09:17,748 --> 00:09:19,455
And then, Pearl said,

290
00:09:19,455 --> 00:09:22,625
every single person in that room looked

291
00:09:22,625 --> 00:09:23,681
at my father.

292
00:09:23,861 --> 00:09:26,183
And my father looked at Frank.

293
00:09:26,363 --> 00:09:28,621
And Frank put me down,

294
00:09:28,621 --> 00:09:30,160
and said, well,

295
00:09:30,160 --> 00:09:32,110
welcome home, Walt.

296
00:09:32,290 --> 00:09:34,020
And held out his hand.

297
00:09:34,370 --> 00:09:35,914
My father shook it.

298
00:09:36,094 --> 00:09:39,031
Pearl said that's what she'll never forget.

299
00:09:39,211 --> 00:09:40,918
He shook his brother's hand.

300
00:09:41,098 --> 00:09:42,776
He said, thank you,

301
00:09:42,776 --> 00:09:45,602
Francis, for looking after them.

302
00:09:45,782 --> 00:09:47,001
And Frank said,

303
00:09:47,001 --> 00:09:48,952
that's what brothers do.

304
00:09:49,132 --> 00:09:51,390
And they stood there holding hands a

305
00:09:51,390 --> 00:09:52,394
second too long,

306
00:09:52,394 --> 00:09:54,024
like two men at the end of

307
00:09:54,024 --> 00:09:56,220
a fight, and then Frank laughed his

308
00:09:56,220 --> 00:09:57,913
big laugh and went to get a

309
00:09:57,913 --> 00:09:58,791
piece of cake.

310
00:09:59,142 --> 00:10:00,226
After the cake,

311
00:10:00,226 --> 00:10:02,684
my father went out to the backyard

312
00:10:02,684 --> 00:10:05,070
by himself and sat on an upturned

313
00:10:05,070 --> 00:10:06,588
bucket by the garden,

314
00:10:06,588 --> 00:10:08,178
under the clothesline,

315
00:10:08,178 --> 00:10:10,926
where my mother's sheets were hanging.

316
00:10:11,106 --> 00:10:13,473
Eddie went out and stood with him

317
00:10:13,473 --> 00:10:14,333
for a while.

318
00:10:14,513 --> 00:10:16,278
Nobody else dared.

319
00:10:16,458 --> 00:10:19,358
Pearl watched them through the kitchen window

320
00:10:19,358 --> 00:10:21,484
while she washed the cake plates,

321
00:10:21,484 --> 00:10:22,902
and she said she knew.

322
00:10:23,082 --> 00:10:25,095
She said she knew right then that

323
00:10:25,095 --> 00:10:26,437
the night wasn't over.

324
00:10:26,787 --> 00:10:29,744
That night, after everyone went home,

325
00:10:29,744 --> 00:10:31,822
my mother put me to bed in

326
00:10:31,822 --> 00:10:33,021
my paper crown.

327
00:10:33,201 --> 00:10:35,365
My father went to the bookshelf in

328
00:10:35,365 --> 00:10:37,401
the front room and took down the

329
00:10:37,401 --> 00:10:38,228
family Bible.

330
00:10:38,408 --> 00:10:40,582
The big black one that had been

331
00:10:40,582 --> 00:10:43,598
in the Miller family since eighteen ninety.

332
00:10:43,778 --> 00:10:45,863
In the front were the pages where

333
00:10:45,863 --> 00:10:48,074
you wrote the births and the deaths

334
00:10:48,074 --> 00:10:49,211
and the marriages.

335
00:10:49,391 --> 00:10:51,653
Pearl had written every one,

336
00:10:51,653 --> 00:10:53,269
in her careful hand.

337
00:10:53,619 --> 00:10:55,130
Walter John Miller,

338
00:10:55,130 --> 00:10:56,482
born March third,

339
00:10:56,482 --> 00:10:57,914
nineteen eighteen.

340
00:10:58,094 --> 00:11:00,028
Francis Joseph Miller,

341
00:11:00,028 --> 00:11:01,610
born June twelfth,

342
00:11:01,610 --> 00:11:03,017
nineteen twenty.

343
00:11:03,197 --> 00:11:04,776
Walter and Dorothy,

344
00:11:04,776 --> 00:11:06,854
married April nineteenth,

345
00:11:06,854 --> 00:11:08,433
nineteen forty-one.

346
00:11:08,613 --> 00:11:10,064
And at the bottom,

347
00:11:10,064 --> 00:11:11,516
in fresh blue ink.

348
00:11:11,696 --> 00:11:13,053
Ruth Ann Miller,

349
00:11:13,053 --> 00:11:14,666
born August eighth,

350
00:11:14,666 --> 00:11:16,363
nineteen forty-four.

351
00:11:16,543 --> 00:11:18,168
Three ten in the morning.

352
00:11:18,348 --> 00:11:20,891
Mansfield General Hospital.

353
00:11:21,241 --> 00:11:23,818
Pearl watched him from the stairs.

354
00:11:23,998 --> 00:11:26,115
He sat in his chair under the

355
00:11:26,115 --> 00:11:28,378
lamp with the Bible open on his

356
00:11:28,378 --> 00:11:30,640
knees, and he didn't move for a

357
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:31,370
long time.

358
00:11:31,550 --> 00:11:33,512
Then she saw his hand.

359
00:11:33,692 --> 00:11:35,237
Resting on the page.

360
00:11:35,417 --> 00:11:37,721
And one finger at a time,

361
00:11:37,721 --> 00:11:39,933
slowly, he was counting.

362
00:11:40,113 --> 00:11:41,297
October.

363
00:11:41,477 --> 00:11:42,626
November.

364
00:11:42,806 --> 00:11:43,863
December.

365
00:11:44,043 --> 00:11:45,111
January.

366
00:11:45,291 --> 00:11:46,719
All the way down.

367
00:11:46,899 --> 00:11:48,745
And then he did it again.

368
00:11:49,095 --> 00:11:51,277
My mother came downstairs.

369
00:11:51,457 --> 00:11:53,338
She stopped in the doorway,

370
00:11:53,338 --> 00:11:55,428
the same way he had stopped in

371
00:11:55,428 --> 00:11:57,100
my doorway that morning.

372
00:11:57,280 --> 00:11:59,010
And he looked up at her.

373
00:11:59,190 --> 00:12:00,420
He didn't shout.

374
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:02,237
He didn't say anything.

375
00:12:02,417 --> 00:12:04,445
He just closed the Bible,

376
00:12:04,445 --> 00:12:07,040
very gently, the way you close a

377
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:09,067
door on a sleeping child,

378
00:12:09,067 --> 00:12:11,338
and put it back on the shelf

379
00:12:11,338 --> 00:12:13,447
exactly where it had been.

380
00:12:13,797 --> 00:12:16,145
Then he took his Army cap off

381
00:12:16,145 --> 00:12:16,873
the hook.

382
00:12:17,053 --> 00:12:18,760
He picked up his cane.

383
00:12:18,940 --> 00:12:21,567
And he walked out the front door,

384
00:12:21,567 --> 00:12:23,080
and down the steps,

385
00:12:23,080 --> 00:12:25,786
and down Maple Street in the dark,

386
00:12:25,786 --> 00:12:28,971
slowly, sideways, the way he walked now.

387
00:12:29,151 --> 00:12:31,465
My mother stood on the porch and

388
00:12:31,465 --> 00:12:32,622
called his name.

389
00:12:32,802 --> 00:12:33,569
Walt.

390
00:12:33,749 --> 00:12:34,526
Walt.

391
00:12:34,706 --> 00:12:36,703
Once, twice.

392
00:12:36,883 --> 00:12:38,799
He didn't turn around.

393
00:12:39,149 --> 00:12:41,584
He didn't come home that night,

394
00:12:41,584 --> 00:12:42,527
sweethearts.

395
00:12:42,707 --> 00:12:44,241
Or the next morning,

396
00:12:44,241 --> 00:12:46,849
when the radio said we had dropped

397
00:12:46,849 --> 00:12:47,923
a second bomb,

398
00:12:47,923 --> 00:12:49,917
on a city called Nagasaki.

399
00:12:50,097 --> 00:12:51,687
Or the night after that.

400
00:12:51,867 --> 00:12:54,578
Tomorrow I'll tell you where my father

401
00:12:54,578 --> 00:12:56,005
went for three days,

402
00:12:56,005 --> 00:12:57,289
and who found him.

403
00:12:57,469 --> 00:12:59,780
And what he brought home with him.

404
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:00,947
Goodnight.

405
00:13:01,127 --> 00:13:04,095
Grandma Ruth will be right here tomorrow

406
00:13:04,095 --> 00:13:04,540
night.
